Annotating
description: select a region, pick an onto - the flow shared by prose, images, maps, and PDFs
- >
  Annotating is **filing a piece of a material under an onto** - one membership, saved **into
  the target itself**: a selected region becomes a fragment on the node (in marklower prose the
  inline token, elsewhere a `yo: fragments:` member), and each applied onto one bookmark line on
  it (the exact shapes: [annotations](*::annotations)) - so it travels with the document,
  persists across reloads, shows up wherever the material is read, and is listed on the onto's
  own page among its members. Its display color is **the applied onto's**: a built-in **pure
  color onto** (`yamlover/ontos/colors/...`) carries an explicit color, and any **named onto**
  gets its stable name-derived hue (the same hue its badges use everywhere). The flow is the
  **same for prose, images, maps, and PDFs**: you **select**, then pick.
- >
  **1. Select** the thing to mark: **prose / PDF** - drag to select text; **image / map** - drag
  a rectangle over the region.
- >
  **2.** A small **picker** pops up by the selection (its cells are [the query
  editor](*::server:query-editor) in pick mode). **Nothing is pre-selected** - a new mark starts
  blank, and the selected text / dragged rectangle stays shown in a **neutral** color while the
  picker is open, so it's clear what you're marking without implying an onto. The controls:
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - '**Color swatches** - the six built-in pure color ontos; click one to apply it.'
  - '**Named chips** - the ontos already on this target (shown **outlined**), those on the same node''s other parts, your **recently-used** ones, and the project''s ontologies; click one to apply it.'
  - '**A path input** - type any onto''s node path and press **Enter** to file the region there. A **bare name** (no path) works too: if no such onto exists yet it is **created on the spot** - appended to the project''s ontologies (the `ontos: *:: ontos` setting in `.yo/settings.yo`, `<root>/ontos` by default) as `<name>: !!<*::yamlover:$defs:onto>` - and then applied.'
  - '**Copy** (prose only) - copies the selected text to the clipboard and creates **no** fragment.'
  - '**Close** (or click anywhere outside) - closes the picker, committing nothing.'
- >
  **3. Picking IS the apply.** Each pick files the region at once and the menu stays open, so
  you can add several; an **applied** onto shows outlined, and clicking it again **removes** the
  membership. Closing without picking leaves the region unfiled - a new selection never files
  itself, and until the first pick nothing has touched the text.
- >
  A new mark appears **immediately** - it doesn't wait for the save round-trip. Saved marks
  render in their onto's color - a highlight under prose, a colored rectangle over an image,
  map, PDF, or DjVu region - and a count shows above the material. The same region can carry
  several memberships - each is its own bookmark line.
- >
  **To re-file or delete a mark, click it.** The same picker reopens in *edit* mode over the
  mark's current ontos (shown outlined): pick another to add, click an outlined one to remove,
  or close to leave it. Removing the last membership removes the fragment - and in prose,
  unwraps its token, restoring the text byte-exact.
- >
  The built-in ontologies ship with the yamlover project (at its root: `ontos/colors`, beside
  `$defs/`); the engine grafts the **self-import key `yamlover`** into every served root -
  including the yamlover project itself - so `/yamlover/ontos/colors/...` and
  `/yamlover/$defs/...` resolve in every project. Whole-node filing needs no fragment at all: a
  bookmark membership on the node itself.
